---
source: claudine/cli/tests/wrap_commands.rs
expression: strip_ansi(&stdout)
---
Wrap Codex CLI with Claudine preflight/env handling

Usage: claudine codex [OPTIONS] [ARGS]...

Arguments:
  [ARGS]...  Arguments forwarded to the wrapped provider CLI

Options:
  -v, --verbose...                   Increase verbosity (-v for verbose, -vv for debug)
  -y, --yolo                         Enable provider-specific YOLO/auto-approval mode
      --include <ENV_NAME>           Preserve this env var even when it matches sensitive-name filters
  -n, --non-interactive              Force provider-specific non-interactive mode [aliases: --ni]
  -m, --model <MODEL>                Override the model used by the provider
  -o, --output <FORMAT>              Set the output format (json, text, stream)
  -s, --system-prompt <PROMPT|FILE>  Set or append a system prompt (string or file path)
  -t, --timeout <SECONDS>            Timeout in seconds (sends SIGTERM then SIGKILL). Only valid with -n
      --dry-run                      Show what would be executed without launching the child
  -q, --quiet                        Show only the header line; suppress env details and info messages
      --silent                       Suppress all Claudine preflight output (header, env, info, warnings)
      --operation <OP>               Set the OPERATION env var for the wrapped session [aliases: --op]
      --sandbox                      Enable provider-specific sandboxing
      --repo                         Use only repo-scoped skills, commands, and agents via a shadow HOME
  -p, --prompt-file <FILE>           Source the initial prompt from a Markdown file (composed with Darkmatter)
      --frontmatter-prompt <FILE>    Inline composition: use frontmatter `prompt` as input, replace body with output [aliases: --fp]
      --compose <FILE>               Chained composition: compose full document and use as prompt (no file mutation)
      --mcp                          Enable Claudine-managed MCP session composition
      --use <ID>                     Activate specific MCP servers by ID or alias (comma-separated)
      --strict                       Treat unresolved or ambiguous MCP tags as hard errors
  -h, --help                         Print help
